  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong></p><p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{GMm}{R^{2}}\"></span> OR <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"mg\"></span>               N</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_03\"><img alt=\"Source markscheme diagram 3\" src=\"asset:fig_03\"/></figure></p>\n<p>Arrow for N and correct labelling ✔ <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{GMm}{R^{2}}\"></span> −N = mω<sup>2</sup>R result follows ✔</p>\n<p>N arrow must be shorter than weight.<br/>Award [0] if there are extra forces or incorrect length arrows in the diagram.<br/>On diagram allow Weight, W, F<sub>g</sub>, gravitational force but not gravity.<br/>Allow rotated diagrams if the surface of the asteroid is shown.<br/>For MP2 the algebra must follow from a diagram with correct length arrows.</p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"b.i\"><p><strong>b.i</strong></p><p>Selection of solar constant, 1360 seen ✔ «Intensity is proportional to <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"d\"></span><sup>−2</sup> so»</p>\n<p>« <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{I}{1360}\"></span> = <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{\\frac{P}{4 π ( 4 {d}^{2} )}}{\\frac{P}{4 π {d}^{2}}}\"></span> » <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"I\"></span> = <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{1360}{4^{2}}\"></span> =«85.0 W m<sup>−2</sup>» ✔</p>\n<p>Allow ECF for MP2 from incorrect solar constant.</p></section><section class=\"markscheme-part\" data-label=\"b.ii\"><p><strong>b.ii</strong></p><p>σT<sup>4 </sup>= 85 OR T = <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{85}{5.67 \\times  10^{-8}}}\"></span> ✔</p>\n<p>T = 196.7 2 ≈ 2.0 × 10<sup>2</sup> «K» ✔</p>\n<p>Award [2] for 140 K, if factor of ¼ is included</p></section>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<section class=\"context\"><p>A probe of mass <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"m\"></span> has landed on the equator of a rotating asteroid of mass <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"M\"></span> and radius <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"R\"></span>.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p>\n<p>The asteroid rotates with angular speed ω.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> </p><p>By drawing a free-body diagram for the probe, show that the normal force, N, on the probe from the asteroid is given by N = m(<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{GM}{R^{2}}\"></span>−ω<sup>2</sup>R).</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_02\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 2\" src=\"asset:fig_02\"/></figure></p><p>[2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> Another probe orbits the Sun.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.i\"><p><strong>b.i</strong> The distance between the probe and the Sun is 4 times the distance between the Earth and the Sun. Show that the intensity of the solar radiation at the surface of the probe is 85 W m<sup>−2</sup>. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b.ii\"><p><strong>b.ii</strong> Estimate the equilibrium temperature of the probe assuming it behaves as a black body. [2]</p></section>"
  },
